      "question": {
        ":question/id": "584e42d1-939f-556c-8cef-ea5c52e25335",
        ":question/problem": "Which of the numbers below belong to $\\mathbb{Q}^{-}$?\n\nSelect all that apply.",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that $\\mathbb{Q}^{-}$ is the set of all negative rational numbers.\n\n- $-\\sqrt{3}$: It is negative, and it is an irrational real number. Therefore, it does *not* belong to $\\mathbb{Q}^{-}$.\n\n- $-0.2=-\\frac{1}{5}$: It is negative, and it is a rational number but not an integer, since it can be written as $-\\frac{1}{5}$. Therefore, it belongs to $\\mathbb{Q}^{-}$.\n\n- $0$: Zero is not negative or positive, and this set does not include zero. Therefore, it does *not* belong to $\\mathbb{Q}^{-}$.\n\n- $-\\sqrt{4}=-2$: It is negative, and it is an integer. Therefore, it belongs to $\\mathbb{Q}^{-}$.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is: $-0.2$; $-\\sqrt{4}$.",
